[{"node":"loopar_crm_menu_content","element":"menu_content","data":{"key":"loopar_crm_menu_content","id":"loopar_crm_menu_content","name":"loopar_crm_menu_content"},"elements":[{"node":"crm-overview","element":"markdown","data":{"label":"Overview","key":"crm-overview","id":"crm-overview","name":"crm-overview","value":"# CRM & Portal

The core app includes a small, shared **CRM** module: one place for the tenant's customers that every app links to, instead of each app keeping its own customer table.

| Entity | What it holds |
|--------|---------------|
| **Customer** | The account — a person or a company: name, type (Individual / Company), status, email, phone, address, notes |
| **Customer Contact** | A person: full name, email, phone, and optionally a **portal user** |
| **Customer Contact Link** | Child row of Customer: which contacts belong to it, their job title, and the **primary** one |
| **Customer External ID** | Child row of Customer: the customer's id in another system (`source` + `external_id`) — Stripe, an accounting tool… |

The rule: **Customer only holds what every app shares.** Each app keeps its own data in its own entity, linked through a `customer` field (a `select` with options `Customer`). A fitness app stores memberships, a services app stores contracts — both point to the same Customer.

On save, a Customer normalizes its name and email, rejects a contact listed twice and keeps exactly one primary contact.
"}},{"node":"crm-helpers","element":"markdown","data":{"label":"Server Helpers","key":"crm-helpers","id":"crm-helpers","name":"crm-helpers","value":"## Server Helpers

Apps use the helpers of the `loopar` app rather than writing Customer rows by hand:

```javascript
import { createCustomer, updateCustomer, customersByName, customerRow,
         externalId, setExternalId, customerByExternalId } from "../../loopar/modules/crm/customers.js";

// Creates the customer and, when it has an email, its primary contact
const customer = await createCustomer({
  customer_name: "Acme Corp", customer_type: "Company",
  email: "billing@acme.com", phone: "555-0100", contact_name: "Jane Doe",
});

await updateCustomer(customer, { city: "Boston" });

// Ids in other systems
await setExternalId(customer, "stripe", "cus_123");
await externalId(customer, "stripe");              // "cus_123"
await customerByExternalId("stripe", "cus_123");   // customer name

// Batch reads for lists
const map = await customersByName(["CUST-0001", "CUST-0002"]); // Map name → row
```

(Adjust the relative path to where your module sits.) The shared fields an app may set are `customer_name`, `customer_type`, `status`, `email`, `phone`, `address`, `city`, `state`, `zip_code`, `country` and `notes`.
"}},{"node":"crm-portal","element":"markdown","data":{"label":"Portal Access","key":"crm-portal","id":"crm-portal","name":"crm-portal","value":"## Portal Access

The **portal** workspace (`/portal/...`) is the signed-in area for people outside the staff — customers checking their orders, members their bookings. A portal user is a **Web user** linked to a Customer Contact:

```
User ──(Customer Contact.user)──▶ Customer Contact ──(Customer.contacts)──▶ Customer(s)
```

Staff create that chain; nothing is inferred from matching emails. Grant access from code:

```javascript
import { grantPortalAccess, revokePortalAccess, ensureCustomerContact,
         customerContacts, primaryContact } from "../../loopar/modules/crm/portal-access.js";

// Creates (or links) the Web user for the contact's email
const { user, created, password } = await grantPortalAccess(contactName, { password: "temporary" });
// password: "temporary" → a readable password returned once, for staff to hand over (must be changed)
//           "unknown"   → nobody knows it; your app signs the user in with its own emailed link

await revokePortalAccess(contactName);          // unlinks the Web user (the user itself is kept)

await ensureCustomerContact(customer, { email: "jane@acme.com", full_name: "Jane Doe" }); // finds or creates the contact
await customerContacts(customer);   // [{ contact, job_title, is_primary }], primary first
```

### What a portal user sees

Records an app exposes to customers carry a `customer` field. Give the portal role a grant with scope **`customer`**: lists are filtered and single records checked against the user's customers. In your own code:

```javascript
import { portalIdentity, requirePortalIdentity } from "loopar";

const { user, contact, customers } = await portalIdentity();     // customers: [] when not linked
const identity = await requirePortalIdentity();                   // 401 / 403 otherwise
```

Private files attached to such records follow the same rule. See [Permissions → Customer scope](/permissions).
